  <div class="post-content"><p>Las Vegas is a city full of opportunity for Latino entrepreneurs. But running a business here comes with tax obligations that many business owners don't fully understand, and that can lead to penalties, IRS debt, and problems that quietly pile up year after year.</p><h2>What taxes does a business in Las Vegas pay?</h2><p>A business in Las Vegas can have obligations at several levels of government:</p><ul><li>Federal taxes: every business reports income to the IRS regardless of which state it operates in. The amount depends on your legal structure and net profits.</li><li>Nevada state taxes: Nevada is one of the most business-friendly states in the US. It has no state tax on personal or corporate income. However, the Modified Business Tax applies to businesses with payroll, and the Commerce Tax applies to businesses with annual revenue over $4 million.</li><li>Sales taxes: if your business sells products in Las Vegas, you must collect and report Clark County sales tax, which is currently one of the highest in the state.</li></ul><h2>When do I need to file taxes if I have a business in Las Vegas?</h2><p>Deadlines depend on your legal structure:</p><ul><li>Sole proprietor and single-member LLC: April 15</li><li>Partnership and multi-member LLC: March 15</li><li>S-Corp and Corporation: March 15</li><li>Estimated quarterly payments: April, June, September, and January</li></ul><p>Filing late triggers automatic IRS penalties even if you don't owe money. Many businesses in Las Vegas pay unnecessary penalties simply because they don't know these dates.</p><h2>What deductions can my Las Vegas business use?</h2><p>A well-advised business can significantly reduce its tax burden using legal deductions. The most common include office or commercial space rent, equipment and technology, vehicles used for business, advertising and marketing, accounting and legal fees, business insurance, and employee salaries.</p><p>Many business owners in Las Vegas pay more taxes than necessary because they don't know all the deductions available for their type of business.</p><h2>What happens if my business owes taxes to the IRS?</h2><p>If your business has outstanding debt with the IRS, the most important thing is not to ignore it. The IRS applies interest and penalties that grow every month. However, there are legal options to resolve the debt, including payment plans, offers in compromise, and in some cases penalty reduction. An experienced accountant can negotiate on your behalf and stop collections.</p><h2>Why your Las Vegas business taxes need professional attention</h2><p>Tax laws change every year, and Nevada has specific rules that don't apply in other states. An accountant who specializes in business taxes in Las Vegas knows exactly what applies to your business, how to take advantage of the state's tax advantages, and how to stay compliant with the IRS all year long.</p><h2>At Guzman Tax Accounting, we handle your business taxes</h2><p>At Guzman Tax Accounting, we specialize in business taxes in Las Vegas. We work with LLCs, S-Corps, sole proprietors, and corporations: all in Spanish, with clear pricing and no surprises. We make sure your business pays exactly what it owes, and nothing more. Call us at (725) 340-6758 or reach out on WhatsApp.</p><a href="/en/consultation" class="btn btn-primary" style="margin-top:8px;">Get a Consultation</a></div>
